{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788369385,"nonce":"5SAD1XFuJnn38zY1qnYXKaueOXw=","title":"Found on the bench, 2026-09-02, proving `a1644d6` acceptance item 2 (\"name the gap\"). `construction {layout_id:\"ly-1\"}` returns:","message":"```json\n\"gaps\": \"System.Collections.Generic.List`1[System.Collections.Generic.Dictionary`2[System.String,System.Object]]\"\n```\n\nA .NET type name where a list of cells should be. **The \"name the gap\" clause of `a1644d6` is unreadable in shipped code**, and it is the clause that turns \"your room is open\" into \"your room is open HERE\".\n\n\n`MiniJson.cs:75` matches `case List\u003cobject\u003e list:` — that exact closed type. `PlaceVerbs.cs:1177` declares:\n\n```csharp\npublic readonly List\u003cDictionary\u003cstring, object\u003e\u003e Gaps = new List\u003cDictionary\u003cstring, object\u003e\u003e();\n```\n\nand `:1215` puts it straight into the envelope. It matches no case, falls to the default, and gets `ToString()`'d. **The serializer does not throw and does not warn** — it produces a syntactically valid JSON string that a consumer will happily read as text.\n\n\nChecked all eight declarations of that type rather than generalising:\n\n- **`PlaceVerbs.cs:1215` — the only one that reaches an envelope directly. This is the bug.**\n- `TradeVerbs` uses `List\u003cobject\u003e`. Fine.\n- `DesignateReach.cs:458` hand-converts: `new List\u003cobject\u003e(v.Unreadable.ToArray())`. **That author hit the trap and worked around it silently**, which is the clearest evidence it is a trap worth closing.\n- `MapDumpVerbs`, `Siting`, `ThingVerbs` are internal accumulators that do not reach an envelope in this shape.\n\n`first_gap` at `:1264` is unaffected — a single `Dictionary` serializes correctly, so a caller reading only `first_gap` sees the right thing while `gaps` beside it is garbage. That divergence is worse than a uniform failure.\n\n\n1. `gaps` serializes as an array of cell objects. Proven on a bench: place a room layout, remove one shell cell, read `construction {layout_id}`, assert `gaps` is a JSON array whose first element has the same shape as `first_gap`.\n2. **`MiniJson` stops silently `ToString()`-ing a type it cannot represent.** Either handle the general case (any `IEnumerable` of representable values) or make the default case loud — an emitted warning naming the offending type and key, so the next occurrence surfaces as a message rather than as plausible-looking text in an envelope. Silence is what made this survive shipping.\n3. If the general case is taken, `DesignateReach.cs:458`'s hand-conversion is no longer needed; leave it or remove it, but say which and why.\n4. A note in `MiniJson`'s header stating which container types are representable, since two authors have now had to discover it by experiment.\n\n\n`a1644d6` is a p0 fix for the defect that starved run `m1-20260901` — the freezer that never enclosed. Its detection half works and was verified on the bench the same evening (`enclosed`, `uses_outdoor_temp`, `unroofed_cells` all correct). **Only the field that tells the agent WHERE the hole is comes back as a type name.** A silent serializer failure in the observation surface is the same class of defect as `eef837a`'s absent-vs-null `ingredient_filter`: the envelope looks answered and is not.","files":null}]}